  "critique": "The parent names broad distinctions but lacks precedence-based routing for recurring near-neighbor errors. It does not force channel identification before state or authorization, and it lacks explicit boundaries for ATM anomalies, compromised versus lost cards, declined versus unrecognized payments, top-up state, and disposable-card purpose versus limits.",
  "evidence": {
    "reviewed_files": ["README.md", "prompting_best_practices.md", "proposal/PROPOSAL_SCHEMA.md", "state/proposer_metadata.json", "state/proposer_readme.json", "state/run_context.json", "state/task_info.json", "state/program_contract.json", "state/proposer_failure_summary.json", "state/proposer_repair_hints.json", "state/proposer_examples.json", "state/reflective_frames.json", "state/parent_payload.json", "state/parent_candidate.json", "state/reflector_input.json", "state/proposal_request.json"],
    "candidate_comparison": "The parent is a concise one-paragraph classifier prompt with an exact-label contract and broad family reminders. Its 36 wins show it handles unambiguous intents such as lost card (train:3), pending top-up (train:22), PIN blocked (train:25), lost phone (train:43), pending cash withdrawal (train:46), and virtual-card failure (train:49). Its 14 losses show that broad reminders fail when card, payment, transfer, ATM, or virtual-card words overlap; this candidate adds ordered channel/status/authorization routing and boundary demonstrations.",
    "failure_patterns": [
